    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d429">

                <pb facs="0453" n="433" xml:id="pg433"/>

                <head>Mr. Burlingame to Mr. Seward</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 86.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Peking,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-06-24"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >June</hi> 24, 1864.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to forward correspondence
                    (marked A and B) with the French minister, Mr. Berthamy, in relation to putting
                    down piracy on the interior waters of China, and particularly on the Yangtse.
                    The plan of Mr. Berthamy proposes to submit to reciprocal search all vessels of
                    a Chinese construction carrying the flag of the treaty powers having ministers
                    at Peking. In my reply to Mr. Berthamy I simply content myself with stating that
                    I have no power to agree to any such plan, and with pointing out one or two
                    difficulties.</p>
                <p>I may say to you, that I hold the plan to be altogether impracticable; and this
                    view I hold with my colleagues representing England and Russia. In the first
                    place, by it we submit our vessels to visitation and search without compensating
                    advantages. Second, we undertake to police Chinese waters, which is an offensive
                    assumption of power, while at the same time it relieves the Chinese government
                    from the performance of its own proper duties. Third, it takes no account of the
                    treaty powers not having ministers at Peking. Fourth, it is not necessary, for
                    anybody may take pirates now. It would lead in practice to the greatest
                    confusion, and would become the fruitful source of quarrels.</p>
                <p>With this brief statement of my views, I submit the correspondence to your better
                    judgment.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>ANSON BURLINGAME.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward,</hi>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, &amp;c., &amp;c.,
                        &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>



